MALIK
Because he kept pulling it back.

The latest red binding has cut a pale groove into the stone. Under it, two older loops have been drawn until they cannot be opened.

At the next stone, the same. Then the next.

Each new loop bites deeper into the same stone. None has enough play to move.

Kai photographs the grooves rather than the forest.

At the ravine overlook, he stops.

Below, through roots: the hidden shrine. The remaining half of the brass fitting still sits in its recess.

A small tremor travels through the seated half. Brass dust falls outward onto the lip.

