And if one of us wants out --

KAI
We all go.

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - PRESSURE CHAMBER - NIGHT

The brothers enter together. Bode remains near the stair. Eno remains upstairs with the family.

On the oldest map, copper routes leave the centered inner door and converge on a worn circle in the floor.

At the circle's center: the stain.

Malik compares the IN and RETURN service plate photograph to two brass guides.

MALIK
Same channels.

He steps to the worn center only after Kai and Ade nod.

The two open brass bands slide along their anchored guide lines.

Not thrown. Not alive. Drawn into position by the loaded geometry of the room.

They close around Malik's forearms with two soft, precise clicks.

Malik feels a pressure in his right shoulder. The WEST line tightens. His left wrist receives a colder answering pull from the river bearing.

His breath catches.

ADE
Say the word.

MALIK
I'm here.

Kai watches an enamel water cup on the worktable. One faint rounded ring.

Malik eases his right arm outward an inch. The WEST guide releases a little copper dust. The river line takes the slack.

On the room's old diagram, a mark that had been hidden by Solomon's newer pencil corrections becomes visible.

MALIK
It isn't a lock.

Kai studies the routes.

KAI
It's carrying something.

Ade stands beside Malik without touching either band.

ADE
Can you get out?

Malik returns both arms to the resting marks. The bands open.

They slide back to their fixed guides.

The skin beneath each band bears a red crescent.

Bode looks at the crescents. Looks away before anybody can see why.

Malik flexes his fingers.

The left hand answers a beat after the right.

ADE
You're done.

MALIK
I know.

He does not step away.

Kai leaves the enamel cup on the worktable and moves to his brother's side.

KAI
You can know something and still not move.

Malik looks at the stained center beneath his shoes.

Then leaves it.

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - STAIRCASE - MOMENTS LATER

Bode waits on the turn where the upper light begins.

Malik stops beside him.

MALIK
You recognized those marks.

BODE
I recognized his arms.

MALIK
You could have told us.

BODE
I could tell you what I saw. I could not tell you what he would not.

Malik climbs past him.

At the top, Ade waits instead of following.

ADE
Was he ever different?

Bode looks back into the depth of the stair.

BODE
Ask her what she kept.

# SEQUENCE 17 - THE MAN BEFORE

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - FRONT ROOM - LATER

Eno brings down an ordinary cardboard box from the top of a cupboard.

ENO
He told me to throw it out.

ADE
And?

ENO
I do not take disposal instructions from frightened men.

Inside: faded dance-hall handbills. A cassette with SOLOMON scratched into the plastic. Photographs that have not been mutilated.

Malik sorts the handbills by date before he reads one.

Ade takes the oldest from the bottom of the stack.

ADE
He had a different shirt for every photograph.

BODE
Most of them belonged to me.

ADE
That tracks.

For a moment, the room almost remembers how to be a family room.

In one, a very young Solomon stands behind a rented turntable, headphones crooked, laughing so hard his face is almost unrecognizable.

Ade looks at the photograph. Then at the stern man on the funeral programme.

ADE
Who is that?

BODE
Your father before he started arriving everywhere early.

Malik studies the picture.

MALIK
Dad didn't dance.

BODE
He did if he thought no one better was watching.

ADE
So exactly like you.

Bode lets the accusation stand.

Eno finds an old portable cassette player in the same cupboard. One battery remains.

She presses PLAY.

A hiss. Then a few seconds of a crowded dance floor, drums and laughter flattened by bad recording.

No voice anybody can identify.

Malik presses STOP.

The click is louder than the music was.

ADE
Why'd you stop it?

MALIK
We don't know what's on it.

ADE
Music.

Malik removes the cassette and lays it squarely beside the funeral programme.

Kai finds another photograph. Their aunt at the worktable, ink on one thumb, eating a slice of orange over a spread of charts. Solomon's hand enters the frame holding the other half.

Her face is turned toward whoever took the picture, mid-chew, annoyed at being interrupted.

BODE
She hated when he put the peel on her pages.

MALIK
Where were you when she died?

Bode takes time.

BODE
Outside town with our uncle.

ADE
And Dad?

BODE
Playing music. He was supposed to come back.

The cassette case opens. Tucked inside: a dance-hall receipt dated the same night as a clipped district clinic record.

Malik notices the times. He does not say them aloud.

He turns the receipt face down.

Kai turns it back.

MALIK
You don't know what the time means.

KAI
He was there.

MALIK
We don't know when he left.

Bode does not rescue him.

MALIK
Uncle.

BODE
He was supposed to leave before the second set.

MALIK
Supposed to.

BODE
He stayed.

Malik places the receipt under his palm.

ADE
He made us show up half an hour early for everything.

MALIK
Because being late matters.

ADE
I didn't say it doesn't.

Kai looks at the two photographs: their aunt laughing with an orange; Solomon laughing behind a turntable.

Kai sets the two photographs beside the unsmiling funeral portrait.

KAI
Did he get there?

BODE
After.

Bode studies the photograph of his sister with the orange.

BODE
He never played another record where I could hear it.

Malik gathers the handbills into a clean stack.

One corner refuses to align.

He tries again.

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - FRONT VERANDA - NIGHT

The courtyard is quieter now. Bedding gone. Half the chairs empty.

Malik stands over the funeral programme and the old dance-hall receipt.

Bode joins him carrying two glasses of water.

He leaves one within reach.

MALIK
He told us people got hurt when you stopped paying attention.

BODE
Sometimes they do.

MALIK
That's not what I mean.

Bode sits.

MALIK
At the funeral I checked the flowers twice. The microphone. The chairs.

He touches the folded programme.

MALIK
I thought that was what he'd given me.

BODE
What?

MALIK
A way to make sure nothing goes wrong.

Bode looks toward the closed outer door at the far end of the passage.

BODE
Did it work for him?

Malik does not drink the water.

MALIK
He stayed.

BODE
Yes.

MALIK
And then he spent the rest of his life making everybody else early.

BODE
He spent it trying not to feel the distance between where he was and where he should have been.

Malik finally looks at his uncle.

MALIK
You make that sound forgivable.

BODE
No.

Bode takes the second glass when Malik does not touch it.

BODE
I make it sound like my brother.

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Eno sets rice before the brothers.

Kai manages three bites. The fourth brings a metallic taste and sudden nausea. He reaches the sink in time.

Ade steadies him with one hand, the other pressed below his own ear.

Malik rubs the red marks on his forearms. Stops when Eno sees.

ENO
Eat what you can. Pain does not become wisdom because you hide it.

Malik takes one bite.

Ade looks at the empty chairs.

ADE
They all know something's wrong.

ENO
They know I asked them to sleep somewhere else.

ADE
And tomorrow?

ENO
Tomorrow they can be angry with me while they are alive.

Kai reaches for his water. The glass slips against his bad hand.

Malik catches it before it tips.

Neither of them mentions the truck.

Malik leaves his hand around the base until Kai has both of his in place.

# SEQUENCE 18 - THE OTHER HALF

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - PRESSURE CHAMBER - NEXT MORNING

The Vancouver carving lies on clean cloth at one end of the worktable.

The active shrine half lies on separate cloth at the other.

Two objects. Related lines. Different hollow treatments. The old carving's rear face bears a heat-darkened crack and worn mounting scars.

Malik finds a dated materials entry after the clinic record.

ORIGINAL REMOVED. NEW CENTER SET. WEST RETURN HELD.

The new entry is Solomon's handwriting. Beneath it, his sister's older, rounder annotation survives on the preceding page.

Leave enough play for the rain.

Malik follows the older line to a diagram Solomon later crossed over three times.

Each correction narrows the space she left between two marks.

KAI
He kept making it smaller.

MALIK
He kept it working.

KAI
Did he?

Malik looks at the band marks on his forearms.

MALIK
Long enough for us to be born.

Kai cannot dismiss that.

KAI
Long enough not to tell us.

Ade stays between the two cloths.

ADE
Both can be true without either of you winning.

ADE
He replaced hers.

KAI
He replaced theirs.

Kai turns the old carving over. A small nick matches the brass silhouette in the old veranda photograph.

MALIK
And carried it all the way to Vancouver.

ADE
He couldn't leave it. Couldn't tell us why.

Nobody turns the old carving into an answer for the broken shrine. Malik wraps it separately again.

Kai compares the old component's mounting scars to the photograph of the fractured fitting.

The angles fail to meet.

He turns the image sideways. Still wrong.

KAI
I thought maybe the old one would fit.

MALIK
Does it?

KAI
No.

Malik slides the older carving farther from the active half.

MALIK
Then it doesn't go near the shrine.

Kai nods and turns the photograph facedown.

On Kai's shrine photograph, the fracture runs through the active component's center. Fresh brass dust lies on the outer lip.

Kai zooms closer. The wood cover was bowed outward before he broke its red thread.

KAI
Something was already pushing.

MALIK
From which side?

Kai cannot answer.

From beneath the inner door: one low, rough vibration.

Not the slower pulse from the pump clearing.

The WEST line pulls taut.

The cup on the table breaks into crowded, jagged ripples.

Ade backs away from the central circle.

ADE
We're done for now.

Malik closes the ledger.

Kai wraps the retired carving and returns it to his camera bag. Ade covers the active shrine half with its separate clean cloth and carries it upstairs.

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - BACK PASSAGE - MOMENTS LATER

The brothers climb together. The outer door closes behind them.

Ade reaches for the practical red hitch.

Malik takes it first and pulls too hard.

The loop cinches flat against the brass hook.

Kai looks at it.

KAI
That's how he left the stones.

Malik's fingers stop.

He works the loop open again. Reties it with enough room to slip a finger underneath.

Nobody congratulates him.

ADE
Can we keep one thing in this house easy to open?

Malik steps away from the door.

# SEQUENCE 19 - TWO RHYTHMS

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - FRONT VERANDA - AFTERNOON

Kai plays the footage from the shrine without looking at its darkest frames.

He has turned the cracked camera screen toward the veranda wall. He watches the pale reflection rather than the recorded ravine.

The first attempt is useless. Traffic on the service road smothers the lower sound.

Kai rewinds.

Ade brings an enamel cup and sets it beside the camera.

ADE
Tell me when you need to stop.

KAI
I'll know.

ADE
That's not what I asked.

Kai looks at him.

KAI
I'll tell you.

On the camera screen: the root hollow. Water under broken leaves. Jagged lines running toward the recess.

A moment later, farther back in the image: one slow circular ring.

Kai isolates the two sounds by listening, not explaining.

First, the low serrated pressure.

Then the rounded note underneath it.

KAI
Listen to which one comes first.

Ade hears it. Malik does not want to.

Kai points to the jagged reflection in the paused image.

KAI
That's when we saw it.

MALIK
So that's the one we keep out.

Kai nods too quickly.

KAI
I think so.

Malik marks the WEST bearing on the back of Solomon's inspection sheet.

Malik folds the sheet as though the mark has settled the question.

Then Ade rewinds past the silhouette.

The rough sound begins first.

The familiar shape appears only after the slower note enters beneath it.

Ade plays the same three seconds again.

ADE
You said that was the one we saw.

Kai studies the reflected screen until the two edges separate.

Blood threads into the white of his right eye.

KAI
I matched the shape to the wrong sound.

Malik looks at the mark he just made over WEST.

MALIK
If we'd used that downstairs --

He does not finish.

He draws one line through the mark rather than darkening it.

MALIK
One sound can change.

KAI
Not while the first one's still there.

MALIK
You were sure the first time.

KAI
I know.

MALIK
If I take that into the room --

KAI
Then don't take my word for it.

Kai turns the camera so both brothers can see the sequence without facing the darkest frame.

ADE
Play it from before.

Kai does.

Rough pressure. Then the slower pulse. Then the familiar impossible shape.

Rough. Rounded. Shape.

Kai plays it again. The order holds.

In an old notebook, their aunt drew the familiar impossible silhouette in charcoal. Tall. Articulated incorrectly. Beside it, a water bowl rendered as concentric, even rings.

On the following page, only a violent field of short diagonal strokes against a closed boundary mark.

The second drawing contains no figure. Only pressure against the boundary.

The drawing of concentric water rings lies beneath the familiar charcoal silhouette.

The sharp diagonal marks crowd against the closed boundary on the opposite page.

Malik compares the sequence to Kai's corrected recording.

MALIK
She separated them.

KAI
Before we did.

ADE
The one we saw.

KAI
It might not be what started this.

The air changes before anyone can improve the sentence.

The water in Eno's drinking glass develops sharp, close ripples. A bruise of dark violet touches the reflection of the brass rim.

Then a faint old-gold line appears in the reflection from the back passage.

Rounded rings meet the jagged pattern. Neither wins.

Kai's right eye reddens again. Malik forgets the start of a breath. Ade grips the veranda rail as his balance folds sideways.

ENO
Inside. Not you three. Everybody else.

She redirects the younger relatives toward the front gate without looking down the passage.

Bode opens the gate. Counts people as they leave.

The two rhythms separate.

Kai wipes blood from the corner of his eye.

KAI
There are two.

# SEQUENCE 20 - THE FIRST CORRECTION

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - PRESSURE CHAMBER - LATE AFTERNOON

The three brothers choose to descend. Bode stays at the top, holding a first-aid box. No family member enters the back passage.

The WEST line vibrates hard enough to raise copper grit from its guide. The river return hangs slack.

Malik takes the central working position.

The two brass bands seat around his already bruised forearms.

MALIK
Read the water.

Kai turns his face from the inner door. Watches the enamel cup instead.

Jagged ripples.

KAI
Right side. Too much.

Malik eases the western guide. A sliver of tension transfers through his shoulders.

Then the serrated pressure strikes again.

An anchored line drops from the dome onto the guide across Malik's left shoulder. Another tightens around his ribs.

The additional lines stay in their anchored tracks, tightening through the only body connected to both working bands.

Malik's fingers go numb around the central index.

ADE
Let go.

MALIK
I can't.

Ade grabs Malik's arm and pulls against the incoming load.

The pressure finds a dead end.

A brutal release throws Ade sideways into the curved stone wall.

His shoulder hits first. Then his ribs. Air leaves him in one thin cry.

Kai turns toward him. The room doubles. He vomits beside the worktable.

The slower rounded pulse enters beneath the rough one.

For an instant, the maintained inner door stands against a depth greater than the chamber can hold.

The wood stays closed. Its shadow seems to recede several feet behind its own frame.

KAI
Malik. The river.

Malik forces his left wrist toward the slack return guide.

The line takes enough load to open one band.

He falls to his knees.

The shoulder line returns along its fixed track. The door remains shut.

Ade tries to stand. His right arm will not lift above his ribs.

Bode reaches the lower stair but does not enter the working circle.

BODE
I'm here.

Malik crawls to Ade using hands that barely close.

MALIK
I'm sorry.

ADE
Try being sorry slower.

He cannot finish the smile.

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - KITCHEN - EVENING

Eno straps Ade's arm against his body with a folded cloth. Each breath costs him.

She cleans the circular abrasions on Malik's forearms. One finger remains without feeling.

Kai rinses his mouth. The red in his right eye has widened.

Ade tries to lift the glass Eno sets beside him.

His right hand reaches the glass. His shoulder cannot bring it up.

He changes to the left.

MALIK
I shouldn't have gone in.

ADE
I went in too.

MALIK
I was the one in the middle.

ADE
You always are.

The line comes out sharper than Ade intended.

Malik takes it without answering.

Kai looks toward the passage.

KAI
If we'd kept the wrong sound --

ENO
You didn't.

KAI
We nearly did.

Eno tightens the sling only enough to hold.

Ade shuts his eyes.

ENO
Then remember how nearly feels.

ENO
No one goes down again until you know what happened to her.

The house gives a low creak toward the western wall.

Kai watches the glass tremble in Ade's good hand.

EXT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - FRONT VERANDA - AFTER DARK

Ade sits alone with his injured arm held close.

The pickup waits beyond the gate. Keys visible on the table beside him.

He could ask Bode to drive.

He does not touch them.

Eno comes out with a fresh cloth and a glass of water.

ADE
I made it worse.

ENO
You tried to pull your brother out.

ADE
That's the part I'm talking about.

Eno sits without looking at his injury first.

ADE
I saw his face and I grabbed him.

ENO
Because you love him.

ADE
That didn't help his breathing.

From inside, Malik's careful cough. Then the sound of Kai rinsing another glass.

Ade listens for both.

ADE
What if the only thing I'm good at is keeping everybody calm while the wrong thing happens?

ENO
Then you would have left when you had the keys.

Ade looks at them.

ENO
You haven't.

She readjusts the cloth at his shoulder.

ADE
It's going to hurt again.

ENO
Yes.

Ade looks toward the gate, then toward the back passage.

He stays.

# SEQUENCE 21 - THE LAST PAGE

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - FRONT ROOM - NIGHT

Eno lays out the clipped district clinic record, old notebooks, the uncut family photograph and the retired brass carving.

Her hand remains on the photograph.

ENO
She was not the worst thing that happened to her.

Kai turns through the aunt's ordinary entries.

A reminder to buy oranges. A complaint about Solomon returning a borrowed record scratched. A neat sketch of the river after rain.

Another line, squeezed into the margin:

Make him eat before he goes.

Malik rests his hand beside the words.

MALIK
She wrote about him like he might listen.

BODE
Sometimes he did.

At the back: a page torn away. The next leaf appears blank.

Kai checks the notebook's spine.

No missing leaf hidden in the binding. No letter tucked into the cover.

He lowers the camera.

KAI
There's nothing here.

ADE
You don't know that yet.

Ade moves to the table. His injured shoulder catches against the chair back.

He stops until the pain passes.

At the bottom corner of the blank page, a diagonal groove interrupts the paper's grain.

Not ink. Pressure.

ADE
She pressed through.

Kai raises the camera. The cracked casing puts a bright flare across half the page.

He shifts left. The lettering disappears.

Shifts right. The flare returns.

Malik turns off the overhead light.

The room falls into partial dark.

Kai's breathing changes.

MALIK
I'm here.

Malik angles the work lamp so Kai does not have to stare directly at the page.

His camera catches indentations the eye misses.

Ade holds the lifted corner down with his good hand.

Malik moves the lamp an inch at a time.

Kai watches the screen instead of the paper.

One fragment appears.

NOT THE ONE.

The rest vanishes under the glare.

Kai lowers the camera before his eye can take any more.

KAI
Again.

Malik changes the angle.

The pressed letters gather into a sentence.

Kai does not invent missing words. He shifts the angle until the pressure marks emerge.

S -- IT WAS NOT THE ONE AT THE DOOR.

IT CAME AFTER.

The last stroke digs into the paper beneath.

An unfinished line below it: OPEN THE RET--

ADE
She wrote to him.

BODE
He never got it.

Bode's voice catches on the smallest fact, not the grandest one.

BODE
He kept looking in the wrong pages.

Malik opens Solomon's index to the same date.

Three separate notes in the wrong ledger. Measurements. Brass. River level.

No record of a letter.

MALIK
He had her words the whole time.

BODE
Not where he knew how to read them.

Malik closes the ledger without aligning its edges.

Malik finds the diagram of the service plate. IN. RETURN.

Solomon's later annotations cover the return path with years of added reinforcement.

The aunt's older line remains visible beneath his pencil.

No entry without an exit.

Ade touches his injured shoulder.

ADE
I pulled him shut.

KAI
You didn't know.

ADE
Neither did she. Not alone.

The retired carving rests on its own cloth beside the notebooks. In the kitchen, the broken shrine half remains separately covered where Ade left it.

# SEQUENCE 22 - WHAT HAPPENED TO HER

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - KITCHEN - LATER

The clinic record is brief. Extensive circumferential compression. Traction injuries. Severe oxygen deprivation. Red fiber embedded beneath both wrists.

The final line gives a time. Nothing else.

Below the report, a collection time.

The dance-hall receipt puts Solomon across town when the first injury was documented.

Malik places the receipt beside the report.

He does not flatten the paper.

MALIK
He could have come home.

Bode stands at the sink.

BODE
Yes.

MALIK
You keep saying that like it makes a difference.

BODE
It does.

MALIK
To who?

Kai looks up from the impression on the notebook page.

KAI
To us.

MALIK
She died.

KAI
I know.

MALIK
He stayed out, she died, and somehow we are still supposed to understand him.

Ade shifts against the sling. The movement costs him.

ADE
Understanding isn't letting him off.

MALIK
Then what is it?

ADE
The part where we don't do it again.

Malik looks at Ade's bound shoulder.

MALIK
I already did.

ADE
Not all the way.

Malik lets the receipt curl between them.

Bode turns off the running tap.

BODE
I was angry with him for years.

MALIK
Were?

Bode dries his hands on a towel already dry.

BODE
Some days I still am.

MALIK
And you never told us.

BODE
He was your father. She was my sister. There wasn't a version that left everybody whole.

Kai looks at the photograph of the aunt holding half an orange.

KAI
There wasn't a version where we got to know her either.

Bode sets the towel down.

His next ledger entry begins in the middle of a sentence. Pencil pressed hard enough to tear the page.

HELD WEST. SET NEW CENTER. RIVER CLOSED.

For years afterward: additional bindings, smaller intervals, fewer open return marks.

MALIK
He thought holding harder meant nobody else would die.

ADE
And nobody else would have to know.

Kai looks at the impression from the missing page.

KAI
She was trying to tell him the thing at the door came after the other pressure.

MALIK
Two directions. One person.

Eno takes the clinical record away when it has done its work.

ENO
Enough of how she died.

She places the orange photograph back in the middle of the table.

ENO
Remember who had to live with it.

Bode looks at his brother's laughing photograph.

BODE
He felt it from town. Ran all the way back. By then there was nothing left he could carry for her.

MALIK
How do you know he felt it?

BODE
Because he stopped in the middle of the street.

Bode glances toward the old cassette case.

BODE
The man who drove him back told me. He said Solomon couldn't get enough air.

Malik touches the center of his own chest.

For the first time, he removes his hand before the next breath arrives.

ENO
She left him something besides the way she died.

Eno taps the older pencil line.

Leave enough play for the rain.

MALIK
He couldn't.

ENO
That does not mean you can't.

Ade looks toward the chamber stairs. One arm held tight against his side.

ADE
We don't go in there one at a time.

Malik studies the return diagram.

MALIK
We don't make one person into the room.

Outside, every insect in the courtyard stops at once.

The glass beside Ade's good hand gives one tight, dark ripple.

A slower ring reaches it from the opposite side.

The patterns strike together and spill a thin line of water over the table.

Eno picks up the clinic record before it gets wet.

No one reaches for the photograph first.

Malik does.

He dries the image of his aunt with the sleeve already marked by the bands.

EXT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - COURTYARD - MOMENTS LATER

Bode walks the remaining relatives to the compound gate in pairs.

One YOUNG WOMAN looks back toward the passage.

ADE
Can you take those blankets?

She turns toward him instead.

His good hand holds out an armful of bedding. His injured arm stays pinned against his side.

YOUNG WOMAN
What happened to you?

ADE
Badly negotiated furniture.

She does not believe him. She takes the blankets anyway.

Eno steps between the young woman and the back passage.

ENO
Go with your uncle.

The gate closes behind them.

Bode remains just inside.

Malik looks at the open route out.

MALIK
Nobody has to come back down.

KAI
That's not the same as nobody going.

ADE
And you're not doing it alone.

Malik looks at the sling.

MALIK
You can't lift that arm.

ADE
I have another one.

Malik turns to Kai.

MALIK
Your eye.

KAI
I don't need to look at the door.

Eno watches them decide without deciding for them.

ENO
If one of you says leave?

MALIK
We all leave.

ADE
Unless one of us can't.

The three brothers sit with the exception.

Bode offers the first-aid box.

BODE
I'll stay where you can call me.

He does not move toward the inner door.

# SEQUENCE 23 - WHAT SOLOMON CLOSED

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - BACK PASSAGE - NIGHT

Eno moves the last relatives out through the front. Bode carries her first-aid box and the pickup keys.

The outer red cord has pulled so tight that its ordinary hitch begins to bite into the hook.

Ade steadies the hook with his good hand. Malik loosens the loop instead of cutting it.

The passage exhales cold, stale air.

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - PRESSURE CHAMBER - CONTINUOUS

The inner door stays physically closed.

Its old-gold threshold line is barely visible beneath layers of Solomon's overdrawn reinforcement.

The copper return channel toward the river has been blocked by an ordinary brass stop, tightened and retightened until the edges are polished.

Malik finds matching entries across thirteen years.

RETURN HELD.

RETURN HELD.

RETURN HELD.

KAI
He could keep it steady alone like that.

MALIK
Until something needed to pass through.

Ade tries to reach the stop. His injured shoulder refuses the movement. He changes angle and uses his good hand instead.

A faint rounded pulse crosses the water in the enamel cup.

The harsh rhythm answers from the WEST line.

Kai does not look at the door. The camera screen, angled toward the cup, becomes his indirect line of sight.

In its reflection, a dark bruised edge pushes inward from the western bearing.

A pale bone-gold response gathers at the maintained threshold.

KAI
The dark one pushes. The other one answers.

MALIK
If we open the return?

KAI
We give the pressure somewhere to go.

The WEST line drives tight enough to crack its copper guide.

Above them, the house lets out the low complaint of an old beam taking too much weight.

Bode calls down from the staircase.

BODE (O.S.)
Everyone is out.

Another strike answers from beneath the land.

The shrine remains broken. The chamber can no longer wait for a cleaner plan.

# SEQUENCE 24 - THE BREACH

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - PRESSURE CHAMBER - CONTINUOUS

The first impact is not a creature.

It is pressure from the wrong bearing.

The WEST line becomes a red bar. Copper grit lifts from the floor channels. In the water cup, every ripple cuts toward the center.

The dark-violet bruise remains only in water and tarnished metal.

Malik steps into the working circle by choice.

The brass bands close over his existing abrasions.

He bites down on the pain and sets both feet against the stone.

MALIK
Kai.

Kai braces the camera against the worktable with both shaking hands.

KAI
West coming in. River still closed.

Ade works at the brass return stop with his good arm. Every turn pulls at the injury along his ribs.

The serrated pressure hits again.

Three anchored lines travel along their fixed guides toward Malik's loaded position.

One catches his shoulder. One draws across his ribs. The third fixes his left ankle to the river bearing.

His body is pulled in three directions at once.

The stain beneath him darkens where sweat falls into the porous stone.

The old stain spreads beneath his feet as another line tightens around his ribs.

KAI
Ade. Now.

Ade gets one turn out of the return stop.

The maintained inner door releases half an inch.

Beyond it: a narrow physical antechamber. Old stone. Another closed outer boundary impossible to place at a useful distance.

For one breath, the familiar impossible silhouette resolves in the controlled threshold between those surfaces.

Too tall. Wrongly articulated. Ancient and unreadable.

It does not reach for Malik.

The slower rounded pressure enters the chamber.

The two rhythms occupy the same water.

Jagged violet-black cuts. Wide old-gold circles. Red load lines carrying the collision through living bodies.

The geometry of the chamber seems to tip. Not the floor itself. The brothers' ability to agree where it is.

Kai's right eye fills red. Blood leaves one nostril. He vomits and keeps the camera aimed at the cup.

Ade's injured shoulder buckles. He nearly falls against the return stop.

Malik tries to breathe.

His chest cannot complete the next instruction.

The rib line tightens.

MALIK
Don't -- touch -- me.

Ade remembers the first correction. His own body thrown against stone. A dead end.

He does not grab Malik.

# SEQUENCE 25 - GIVE ME ONE SIDE

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - PRESSURE CHAMBER - CONTINUOUS

Ade follows the return line with his eyes.

From Malik's left wrist. Across a copper guide. Through the brass stop. Toward the river channel in the floor.

The line has an entrance.

It has no exit.

ADE
Malik.

Malik's hands have gone pale inside the bands. His left fingers cannot close.

Ade puts his good arm beneath the anchored return line. With his injured side, he forces the brass stop open another fraction.

He makes himself part of the route, not a wall across it.

ADE
Give me one side.

Malik looks at Ade's outstretched arm.

His fingers tighten once around both bands.

Then he loosens his left grip.

The pressure moves into Ade.

His injured ribs seize. Blood runs from his nose. He lets the line travel over his forearm and out toward the open river guide.

ADE
Don't hold it. Let it through.

The shoulder line on Malik loses enough tension for one arm to move.

Kai watches only the reflected water.

The dark pattern forces inward from WEST. The slower gold rings answer from the threshold.

Between them, the old center index remains turned toward Solomon's closed bearing.

KAI
Center. Not west. The middle.

Malik reaches with two numb fingers.

Misses.

The inner door opens another breath's width.

The familiar form becomes almost coherent.

No one looks directly at it.

Kai loses his balance. His shoulder strikes the worktable. The enamel cup begins to fall.

He catches it against his chest. Water spills down his shirt. Enough remains to read.

KAI
Again.

Ade's good arm shakes beneath the moving return load.

ADE
I have this side.

Malik releases the instinct to hold both.

He rotates the center index one measured quarter turn.

The return channel opens.

The chamber takes a breath through all three of them.

The rough pressure drives hard once more against the western line.

The rounded answering pulse does not strike it like a weapon. It restores the line between the controlled threshold and what has no right to enter it.

In the enamel cup, the jagged violet cuts are pressed outward to the rim.

The dark reflection vanishes beneath the lip.

The old-gold ring widens once.

The familiar silhouette recedes into the antechamber, its final articulation bending where the stone cannot contain it.

The inner door settles closed.

The red lines release along their fixed guides.

Ade hits the floor on his uninjured side.

Kai drops beside the worktable, retching without anything left to bring up.

The brass bands open.

Malik collapses across the old stain.

For one unbearable moment, his chest does not move.

Ade drags himself across the stone with one arm.

ADE
Malik.

No response.

Kai reaches Malik's face with a shaking hand.

KAI
Malik.

One ragged breath begins.

Then another.

Above them, Bode starts down the stair but stops at the edge of the chamber, waiting for them to call him in.

BODE
Tell me.

Ade looks at his brothers. At the lines now carrying a slow, even load.

ADE
We're here.

# SEQUENCE 26 - A WAY HOME

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - KITCHEN - PRE-DAWN

Eno cleans blood from Kai's cheek. His hand still shakes too badly to hold the cloth.

She secures Ade's arm against his chest again. Every knot leaves a loop that can be loosened.

Malik sits bent over his own breathing. Red welts cross his forearms and ribs. Two fingers remain numb.

The broken brass half rests beneath its ordinary kitchen cloth.

The retired Vancouver carving remains wrapped inside Kai's camera bag. It will not be used as a substitute.

KAI
The other half is still there.

MALIK
Then we're not finished.

Eno looks at what the room has done to each of them.

ENO
You do not walk there like this.

Bode sets the pickup keys on the table.

BODE
The pump clearing. After that, the path.

Eno brings clean red cotton from Solomon's carefully labeled materials. She places it beside the broken half.

ENO
Bring everybody back.

EXT. WESTERN SERVICE TRACK - DAWN

Bode drives slowly enough that every rut can be seen before it reaches them.

Ade rides in front, his shoulder bound. Kai braces the wrapped brass half between both palms. Malik sits beside him, unable to make a fist.

At the irrigation gate, Bode stops. He remains with the truck.

The brothers take the maintained footpath beyond the pump clearing together.

EXT. WESTERN BOUNDARY PATH - CONTINUOUS

The over-tight red loops still bite into the stones. The work after this morning will not repair itself.

Ade leans on Malik with the side that does not hurt as badly. Malik accepts his weight without trying to carry all of it. Kai stops twice to steady the world.

They reach the ravine by the established path.

EXT. HIDDEN SHRINE - DAWN

The remaining half waits in the recess.

Kai unwraps its missing piece. His fingers shake. Malik steadies his wrist without taking the brass away.

Ade supports the old wooden cover against his good shoulder.

Kai aligns the fracture.

The halves meet. Still cracked. The fitting settles back into its original working place.

Malik guides the clean red cotton around the cover with fingers that can barely feel it.

Ade catches the other end.

Kai shows them the old loop from his first photograph.

Together, they tie a binding that holds -- and can still be opened.

Nothing announces approval.

One insect resumes.

Then another.

Water moves somewhere beyond the trees.

Malik lets go of the knot first.

Ade keeps his hand beneath Malik's for one more breath.

Kai closes his camera bag around the old carving.

Three brothers stand before the same broken thing, no longer making one body carry all of it.

They turn toward the path home together.

FADE OUT.
